think Read the filmstrip trio: open-image paints the whole strip (refuses FilmstripTooWide past the 256-col map ceiling), open-image tap-to-jump, open-image drag-to-scrub — all paint the WHOLE strip. open-image added filmstrip_window(first, count) but wired it to no interaction, forcing no decision about WHICH window to show.
think the Long Return Lila: the durable crux is a window that FOLLOWS the cursor so a long history is paintable at every step. The pixel-level windowed-finger classification is a smaller later rung (open-image) — named, not taken this lap.
think Designed FilmstripTrack: owns one FilmstripJump + one number win_bands; one pure fn window_first centers the cursor and clamps to edges (first = clamp(position − win_bands/2, 0, steps − win_bands)). The clamp proves the promise — the cursor always lands inside the window — so unlike open-image's fixed window the marker NEVER blanks.
